## Why This Is Asked
Tests for debounce ensure timing behavior and last-argument handling are correct.

## Key Concepts
- Debounce mechanics, timers, last-args capture
- Jest fake timers or equivalent
- Edge cases with rapid invocations

## Code Example
```javascript
function debounce(fn, wait) {
  let t;
  return function(...args) {
    clearTimeout(t);
    t = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), wait);
  };
}

test('debounce calls only once with last args', () => {
  jest.useFakeTimers();
  const mock = jest.fn();
  const debounced = debounce(mock, 100);
  debounced(1);
  debounced(2);
  debounced(3);
  jest.advanceTimersByTime(100);
  expect(mock).toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1);
  expect(mock).toHaveBeenCalledWith(3);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test debounce in a React component?
- How do you handle cleanup on unmount?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end, fault-tolerant data pipelines and reason about exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics in real-time systems.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing of streaming pipelines
- Idempotency and deduplication strategies
- Replay, backpressure, and regional outage resilience
- Metrics: data loss, duplicates, latency, SLAs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple dedup: track seen event_ids to skip duplicates in sinks
const seen = new Set();
function process(event){
  if(seen.has(event.event_id)) return false; // duplicate
  seen.add(event.event_id);
  // write to sink
  return true;
}
```

